{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3978","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3978","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3978\",\"parent_revision\":\"5ab0dc8c412c11f558bd280bae7d5d0060d425be\",\"root_revision\":\"259dcd5c63f7c347ed6b65d8dd5d44c356f7adbf5504c5b0e655ea29877c66f5\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: in-progress\\nadded: 2026-09-27\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA REST 403 for the primary rate limit ('API rate limit exceeded', RateLimitExceededException, often without a Retry-After header) is classified NON_RETRYABLE by sync_state._classify_github_exception. Sync then goes OFFLINE and MCP responses report retryable: false while the limit is only temporary. Found by Codex on #3968 (PRRT_kwDOQGimCs6mbw5i); the behaviour predates #3968.\\n\\nUser-provided context: classify RateLimitExceededException as retryable before the generic 403 rule, and take the delay from x-ratelimit-reset when Retry-After is absent.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T22:11:29.438897Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: A REST 403 for the primary rate limit ('API rate limit exceeded', RateLimitExceededException, often without a Retry-After header) is classified NON_RETRYABLE by sync_state._classify_github_exception.\\nevidence: Source code inspection of _classify_github_exception (backlog_core/sync_state.py lines 352-379, commit 6befa35e3 onward): the 403 branch checks for Retry-After header and \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" message marker, but contains no check for exhausted x-ratelimit-remaining header or \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\" message text. When neither condition is true, it returns NON_RETRYABLE (line 376). This classification applies to any 403 lacking a Retry-After header and without \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" in the message, including primary rate-limit exhaustions identified by x-ratelimit-remaining==0. The gh_client._is_rate_limited helper (lines 892-916) correctly identifies primary rate limits via RateLimitExceededException or x-ratelimit-remaining exhaustion for GraphQL paths, but sync_state does not use this check for REST responses.\\nsource: file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py:352-379 (lines 371-376 showing 403 classification logic)\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: the behaviour predates #3968\\nevidence: Git history shows commit 6befa35e3 (\\\"fix(dh): stop GitHub requests hanging and classify rate limits as retryable (#3968)\\\") introduced logic checking for \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" in message and Retry-After header. Before that commit (parent 6befa35e3~1), 403 classification was: `headers: dict[str, str] = exc.headers or {}; return SyncErrorKind.RETRYABLE if \\\"Retry-After\\\" in headers else SyncErrorKind.NON_RETRYABLE`. The current code (unchanged since 6befa35e3) still does not handle primary-rate-limit 403s specifically, confirming the original behavior predates #3968. The issue was not introduced by #3968 but rather coexisted with its secondary-rate-limit fix.\\nsource: git show 6befa35e3~1:plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py sed -n '330,340p' (pre-#3968 code); git log --oneline shows 6befa35e3 is the #3968 commit; current code identical in 403 handling\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: Found by Codex on #3968 (PRRT_kwDOQGimCs6mbw5i)\\nevidence: The reference \\\"PRRT_kwDOQGimCs6mbw5i\\\" is a GitHub PR discussion thread ID, not a standalone issue. Attempting to read issue #3968 returns 404 (\\\"Could not resolve to an Issue with the number of 3968\\\"). Commit 6befa35e3 exists with message \\\"fix(dh): stop GitHub requests hanging and classify rate limits as retryable (#3968)\\\" and addresses secondary, not primary, rate limits. Without access to the PR discussion thread content or a linked issue, I cannot verify whether Codex found the primary-rate-limit 403 problem within #3968 discussions or whether this is a newly identified related issue. The claim statement is unclear: does \\\"found by Codex on #3968\\\" mean discovered while working on that issue, or discovered and reported in that thread?\\nsource: unavailable \u2014 GitHub PR discussion thread ID requires authentication; issue #3968 resolves to PR commit 6befa35e3 (secondary rate limit fix, not primary)\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Sync then goes OFFLINE and MCP responses report retryable: false while the limit is only temporary.\\nevidence: SyncStatus.OFFLINE state is entered when a sync failure is classified NON_RETRYABLE (sync_engine delegates classification to classify_sync_error, which uses _classify_github_exception for GithubException). When a primary-rate-limit 403 without Retry-After header is classified NON_RETRYABLE, sync transitions to OFFLINE (sync_state.py lines 85-86 and state machine behavior). MCP responses report retryable field based on SyncErrorKind classification through server._failure_verdict, propagating the NON_RETRYABLE verdict. The rate limit is temporary by GitHub's definition (temporary exhaustion of remaining quota), but the code treats it as permanent.\\nsource: file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py:85-93 (SyncStatus enum), server.py _failure_verdict, classify_sync_error function logic\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T22:24:31.795915Z</sub>\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Does GitHub document that a primary-rate-limit 403 (and 429) response carries x-ratelimit-remaining: 0 and x-ratelimit-reset (UTC epoch seconds), with or without Retry-After?\\nevidence: GitHub's REST API documentation (https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api) states: \\\"When you exceed your primary rate limit, you will receive a 403 or 429 response, and the x-ratelimit-remaining header will be 0.\\\" The documentation further specifies that x-ratelimit-reset indicates when the window resets. For primary rate limits, the guidance is \\\"You should not retry your request until after the time specified by the x-ratelimit-reset header.\\\" The Retry-After header is documented only for secondary rate limits: \\\"If the retry-after response header is present, you should not retry your request until after that many seconds has elapsed.\\\" This implies Retry-After is not included in primary rate limit 403/429 responses. PyGithub's Requester.createException (github/Requester.py line 964) passes all response headers directly to GithubException constructor, confirming the full header dict (including x-ratelimit-remaining, x-ratelimit-reset) is available on the exception object.\\nsource: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api (GitHub REST API documentation); file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/Requester.py:964\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Does the MCP retryable/retry_after contract and the background sync retry path consume the classifier output as the Impact Radius states?\\nevidence: backlog_core/sync_engine.py line 221 calls retry_after_seconds(exc) and stores result in state.retry_after; line 259 uses max(_compute_backoff_delay(attempt), state.retry_after or 0.0) to compute retry delay. backlog_core/server.py lines 308-323 implement _failure_verdict which calls _retryable(exc) and retry_after_seconds(exc) to build MCP error response fields. _retryable (lines 261-305) consumes classify_github_failure(exc) which in turn calls _classify_github_exception for direct GithubException or walks __cause__ chain. Both sync engine and MCP server paths route through these same classifier functions, as documented in the Impact Radius change frame. The MCP response includes retryable (boolean) and retry_after (seconds) fields built directly from these classifier outputs (lines 320-322).\\nsource: file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_engine.py:219-262; file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:260-323\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: Does the GraphQL path (gh_client._graphql_request / GitHubRateLimitedError) already take a delay from x-ratelimit-reset, or only from Retry-After?\\nevidence: backlog_core/gh_client.py line 967 constructs GitHubRateLimitedError with retry_after=parse_retry_after_header(exc.headers). The parse_retry_after_header function reads the Retry-After header value from headers and converts it to seconds. No fallback to x-ratelimit-reset is present in this code path. The function only checks exc.headers for \\\"retry-after\\\" key (case-insensitive due to PyGithub lowercasing header names); it does not check for \\\"x-ratelimit-reset\\\". This means the GraphQL path only uses Retry-After, not x-ratelimit-reset, even though x-ratelimit-reset is available in exc.headers for primary rate limit errors.\\nsource: file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py:965-967; file:///Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py:433-460 (parse_retry_after_header function)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T22:01:42.736351Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-27T22:30:52.706281Z \u2014 rtica-assessor supersedes the RT-ICA snapshot</summary>\\n\\n**Goal**: Reclassify GitHub primary-rate-limit 403 responses as retryable and extract the retry delay from x-ratelimit-reset when Retry-After is absent, preventing sync from incorrectly transitioning to OFFLINE for temporary limits.\\nDate: 2026-09-28\\n**Assessed**: 2026-09-28T20:45:00Z\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n| # | Condition | State | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Current behavior: RateLimitExceededException is classified NON_RETRYABLE by sync_state._classify_github_exception | AVAILABLE | Fact-Check entry 1 (VERIFIED): source co