nd_status` (~lines 2043-2048): the `except GithubException` branch treats any `exc.status == 403` as `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED` with no check for rate-limit-specific headers or message, contradicting `sync_state._classify_github_exception`'s permission-denied-vs-rate-limit split (issue A).\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_client.py \u2014 `make_github_client` (~line 912): constructs `Github(auth=..., base_url=..., timeout=timeout)` with no `retry=` argument, so PyGithub falls back to its `Github.default_retry` (a `GithubRetry` instance; see `github/GithubRetry.py` in the installed PyGithub package), whose `increment()` sleeps synchronously up to `X-RateLimit-Reset + 1s` on a primary-limit 403 before returning control to the caller (issue B).\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py \u2014 `_build_over_budget_view` (~line 2213) returns `result.number` unmodified; `_build_compact_manifest` (~lines 2081-2085, the `summary=True` path) regex-parses `result.issue` as a fallback when `result.number` is `None`, but `_build_over_budget_view` has no equivalent fallback \u2014 this is the symptom site for issue C (reproduced live on #3978 itself: `summary=False` over-budget call returned `\\\"number\\\": null`).\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py \u2014 `view_result_from_local_item` (~lines 1425-1440): constructs `ViewItemResult` with `issue=item.issue` (string form, e.g. `\\\"#3978\\\"`) but never sets the `number` field, leaving it at its `int | None = None` default \u2014 this is the root cause for issue C.\\n- plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py \u2014 `ViewItemResult.number` (~line 1812): `int | None = None`, the field every downstream response builder reads for issue C.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193487Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py (`_is_rate_limited` \u2014 already correctly classifies primary-limit signals for the GraphQL path) |\\n| Prior work | commit 6befa35e3 (#3968, merged) \u2014 established the RETRYABLE-with-delay pattern for secondary rate limits that this item extends to primary limits |\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md \u00a7 Error Handling Pattern (~lines 235-243) |\\n| Docs | plugins/development-harness/AGENTS.md \u00a7 State Management (~lines 184-193) |\\n| Docs | GitHub REST API rate-limit documentation (x-ratelimit-remaining / x-ratelimit-reset semantics, Retry-After only documented for secondary limits) |\\n| Backlog | #3968, #3969, #3975 (all merged) \u2014 the GitHub-robustness session this finding came from |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193420Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: background sync (`sync_engine._attempt_sync`) and every backlog MCP tool caller (agents calling `backlog_list`, `backlog_view`, `sync_now`, etc.) during a primary-quota rate-limit window \u2014 sync parks in `OFFLINE` and requires manual `sync_now` recovery for a condition that resolves itself on GitHub's own schedule; tool callers get `retryable: false` with no `retry_after` hint and no basis to just wait.\\n- Bottleneck: `sync_state._classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch is the single classification chokepoint feeding 8+ downstream call sites (`sync_engine.py`, `server.py`, `gh_client.py` x2, `backends/github_backend.py` x6), so this one misclassification propagates to every code path that can see a GitHub 403.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T22:11:46.490644Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-28T08:56:44.865626Z \u2014 impact analysis refreshed</summary>\\n\\nSCOPE_EXPANSION: Found 2 systems outside the starting impact set - the MCP tool retry-verdict contract and the multi-call-site GitHub backend consumers that all route through the same classifier. This expands fact-check scope to: `backlog_core/server.py::_retryable`/`_failure_verdict` (MCP `retryable`/`retry_after` response fields), `backlog_core/sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync` (background sync retry/backoff), `backlog_core/gh_client.py` (2 call sites), `backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` (6 call sites).\\nIMPACT_RADIUS_COMPLETE: Written to item #3978. Overall risk: MEDIUM. Highest-risk: backlog_core/sync_state.py (classifier + retry-delay source of truth); backlog_core/sync_engine.py (retry/backoff timing).\\n\\n### Change Frame\\n- Baseline: `sync_state._classify_github_exception`'s 403 branch treats any REST 403 as retryable only when it carries a `Retry-After` header or a \\\"secondary rate limit\\\" message substring. A primary-quota 403 (`RateLimitExceededException`, \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\", typically no `Retry-After`) falls through to NON_RETRYABLE. `retry_after_seconds` only reads the `Retry-After` header/GraphQL `retry_after`; it has no fallback to `x-ratelimit-reset`.\\n- Delta: classify `RateLimitExceededException` (or an equivalent primary-limit signal) as RETRYABLE ahead of the generic 403 rule, and derive a retry delay from the `x-ratelimit-reset` header (an epoch timestamp requiring a `reset - now` conversion) when `Retry-After` is absent.\\n- Intended outcome: a primary-quota 403 keeps the background sync retrying (with a delay long enough not to hit the same limit again) instead of parking in OFFLINE, and MCP tool error responses report `retryable: true` with a `retry_after` hint for the same failure.\\n- Non-goals: the GraphQL rate-limit path (`gh_client._graphql_request`'s `GitHubRateLimitedError` construction), the secondary-rate-limit branch, and `gh_client.probe_backend_status`'s independent 403-handling are not named by the item and are called out separately below.\\n- Time horizon and rollback boundary: pure classification/delay-computation logic, no schema or stored-state migration; revertible by reverting the diff. The only carried state is in