b_backend.py:294-556; backlog_core/gh_client.py:965-974,2288-2292 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: existing parametrized suites in these files re-run with a `RateLimitExceededException` fixture added.\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py::_classify_github_exception` - primary edit site; must check for `RateLimitExceededException` (or a message/header-based primary-limit signal) before the existing `has_retry_after or secondary-marker` check, without changing the existing secondary-limit or generic-403 (permission-denied) verdicts. | Risk: HIGH | Why: sole source of truth for every downstream consumer listed below; a mis-ordered or over-broad check would reclassify ordinary permission-denied 403s as retryable.\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py::retry_after_seconds` (and/or a new helper) - needs an `x-ratelimit-reset` fallback that converts an epoch timestamp to a `max(0, reset - now)` delay; must not regress the existing `Retry-After`-first precedence or the GraphQL `GitHubRateLimitedError.retry_after` path. | Risk: HIGH | Why: this value flows unfiltered into `sync_engine`'s backoff `max()` and into every MCP tool's `retry_after` field; a sign or unit error (seconds vs epoch) produces a multi-decade sleep or a needless empty retry.\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py::classify_sync_error` docstring (the \\\"classification table\\\" comment, lines ~504-540) - documents the exact 403 rules being changed and will drift the moment the code changes if not updated in the same edit. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: this docstring is the nearest thing to a design record for this logic (no separate design doc file exists); a stale table misleads the next change here the same way the current gap did.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n- `backlog_core/sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync` - consumes `classify_sync_error` and `retry_after_seconds` directly to decide OFFLINE vs retry-with-backoff. | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is the exact user-visible bug (sync going OFFLINE on a temporary limit); it is also where a bad delay value would manifest as a stalled or thrashing retry loop.\\n- `backlog_core/server.py::_retryable`, `_failure_verdict` - consumes `classify_github_failure` and `retry_after_seconds` to build every MCP tool's `retryable`/`retry_after` error fields. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: agents calling backlog MCP tools branch on this field; flipping it from false to true changes caller retry behavior across the whole tool surface, not just sync.\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py::_graphql_request` (non-rate-limited except-arm, line 972) and the issue-status-map builder (line 2291) - both call `classify_sync_error` on a caught `GithubException`/`BacklogError` to set `BackendUnavailableError.retryable`. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: same verdict flip, reached from GraphQL and REST issue-status code paths respectively.\\n- `backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py::fetch_snapshot`, `fetch_page`, `search_issues_by_title`, `fetch_issue_titles`, `confirm_issues_reachable` - six call sites combining `classify_github_failure`/`classify_sync_error` to set a `retryable` flag on backend-level failures. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: broadest fan-out of consumers; each is an independent read/write path that can now surface `retryable: true` for a primary-limit 403 it previously reported as permanent.\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py::probe_backend_status` (line 2047) - independently treats *any* 403 as `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED`, without going through the classifier. | Risk: LOW | Why: not on the call path being changed, but easy to mistake for the same logic during review; excluded from the impact set below on that basis.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py` - the tool-boundary retry-verdict suite; needs a new `_TRIP_FAILED`-style case for a `RateLimitExceededException` (or equivalent 403 with no `Retry-After`/no secondary-limit wording). | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: this is the canonical regression home for \\\"a trip failure vs a call failure\\\" and currently has no case distinguishing primary-limit from permission-denied 403s.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` (line ~593) - constructs a plain `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})`, not `RateLimitExceededException`; verified this stays NON_RETRYABLE under the intended fix (message has no rate-limit wording, isinstance check does not match). | Risk: LOW | Why: confirmed non-conflicting by evidence, but the fix must preserve this \u2014 it is the permission-denied case the classifier still needs to reject.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` - pins the existing secondary-limit (message-substring) branch; the new primary-limit branch must be additive, not reordering this one. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: two branches now share the 403 arm; a reordering mistake could make one shadow the other.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` - pins that an environment-wide GraphQL-refusal 403 (no rate-limit wording, plain `GithubException`, not `RateLimitExceededException`) stays NON_RETRYABLE; verified by evidence it is unaffected by an isinstance-based primary-limit check. | Risk: LOW | Why: confirmed non-conflicting, but worth re-running given it exercises the same 403 branch.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py` - the `Retry-After`/`retry_after_seconds` suite; needs new cases for the `x-ratelimit-reset` fallback (present/absent, casing, non-numeric, already-past timestamp). | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is the only place today asserting `retry_after_seconds` semantics; the new delay-conversion logic (epoch to seconds-from-now) is new arithmetic with no existing coverage.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py` - GraphQL `RATE_LIMITED`/`_is_rate_limited` suite; unaffected by this REST-classifier change but shares the \\\"primary vs secondary\\\" vocabulary and should be checked for drift if a shared helper is introduced. | Risk: LOW | Why: separate code path (`gh_client._is_rate_limited`), not modified by this item's stated scope.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_sync_state_classify.py` - `ContentProviderError`/`__cause__`-chain suite; delegates to `classify_github_failure` internally, so a primary-limit 403 wrapped in `ContentUnavailableError` now also flips to RETRYABLE. | Risk: LOW | Why: existing chain-walking logic is unchanged, only the leaf classification; no new branch needed here but worth one added case for completeness.\\n\\n### Documentation\\nNone identified. No file under `docs/` documents this classification table or retry-delay precedence; the nearest thing to documentation is the `classify_sync_error` docstring itself, already listed under Code - Producers.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\nNone identified. No workflow, pre-commit, or deployment config encodes GitHub rate-limit classification.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\nNone identified. No skill or agent file references `_classify_github_exception`, `classify_sync_error`, or GitHub rate-limit retry semantics.\\n\\n### Data / State / Runtime\\n- `SyncState.status`/`offline_reason` (in-memory process singleton) - a primary-quota 403 no longer transitions the background sync to `OFFLINE`; it instead increments `retry_count` and sleeps for a GitHub-supplied delay. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: `sync_now`'s own tool contract (this session's tool list) states it \\\"clears the state and attempts a fresh sync\\\" when the last sync entered OFFLINE \u2014 that manual-recovery path now triggers less often for this specific cause, which is the intended fix, not a defect, but changes what an operator sees in `sync_status` for a rate-limited environment (extended retrying instead of a terminal OFFLINE).\\n\\n### Models / Prompts / Context\\nNone identified. No LLM prompt, tool description text, or context-assembly logic depends on this classification's specific verdict beyond the boolean/optional-float fields already covered above.\\n\\n### People / Process / Controls\\n- Operator/agent expectation that `sync_status.offline_reason` signals a permanent, action-requiring failure - a primary-quota 403 will now resolve itself via retry rather than requiring a manual `sync_now` call. | Risk: LOW | Why: this is the intended behavior change (the item's whole premise); flagged so a reviewer confirms no downstream alerting or runbook assumes \\\"OFFLINE means call sync_now\\\" is the *only* path back from a rate limit, since that path now also self-heals silently.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py::_classify_github_exception` | Role: producer | Propagation: direct edit site -> every consumer below | Outcome: primary-limit 403 reclassified NON_RETRYABLE to RETRYABLE | Stakeholder: all GitHub-backend callers | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: sync_state.py:352-379 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: unit test with `RateLimitExceededException`, no `Retry-After`, no \\\"secondary\\\" wording | Action: modify | Risk: HIGH | pattern: '_classify_github_exception' | pattern_count: 3\\n- `backlog_core/sync_state.py::retry_after_seconds` | Role: producer | Propagation: new x-ratelimit-reset fallback -> sync_engine backoff, server retry_after field | Outcome: retry delay now derivable without a Retry-After header | Stakeholder: sync loop timing, MCP callers | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: sync_state.py:433-460 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: test asserting epoch-to-delay conversion and its bounds | Action: modify | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'retry_after_seconds' | pattern_count: 8\\n- `backlog_core/sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync` | Role: consumer | Propagation: classify_sync_error/retry_after_seconds -> OFFLINE-vs-retry decision and backoff delay | Outcome: primary-limit 403 retries with a GitHub-derived delay instead of going OFFLINE | Stakeholder: background sync availability | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: sync_engine.py:196-221,257-260 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: integration test simulating a primary-limit 403 across one `_sync_loop` iteration | Action: verify | Risk: HIGH\\n- `backlog_core/server.py::_retryable / _failure_verdict` | Role: consumer | Propagation: classify_github_failure/retry_after_seconds -> MCP tool error payload fields | Outcome: `retryable` flips false to true, `retry_after` appears, for the same failure | Stakeholder: MCP tool callers (agents) | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: server.py:260-323 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: `test_retryable_classification.py` case for this exception | Action: verify | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `backlog_core/gh_client.py` (2 call sites: `_graphql_request` non-rate-limited branch; issue-status-map builder) | Role: consumer | Propagation: classify_sync_error -> BackendUnavailableError.retryable | Outcome: same verdict flip surfaced through a different exception wrapper | Stakeholder: GraphQL and REST issue-status callers | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: gh_client.py:964-974,2288-2292 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: existing GithubException-classification tests re-run with the new fixture | Action: verify | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` (6 call sites: fetch_snapshot, fetch_page, search_issues_by_title, fetch_issue_titles, confirm_issues_reachable) | Role: consumer | Propagation: classify_github_failure/classify_sync_error -> per-call retryable flag | Outcome: same verdict flip across every listed backend read path | Stakeholder: GitHub backend read/write operations | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: github_backend.py:294-556 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: existing per-method test suites re-run with the new fixture | Action: verify | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_retryable_classification.py` | Role: test | Propagation: asserts _retryable's verdict table | Outcome: needs a new parametrized case | Stakeholder: regression coverage | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: file body, `_TRIP_FAILED` list | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: add case, run suite | Action: extend | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py` | Role: test | Propagation: asserts retry_after_seconds/parse_retry_after_header behavior | Outcome: needs new x-ratelimit-reset fallback cases | Stakeholder: regression coverage for the new delay arithmetic | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: file body | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: add cases (present/absent header, non-numeric, past timestamp), run suite | Action: extend | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` | Role: test | Propagation: pins existing secondary-limit branch ordering | Outcome: must still pass unchanged after the new branch is added | Stakeholder: regression coverage | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: file body | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: run suite unmodified | Action: verify | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_startup_sync.py` | Role: test | Propagation: pins 401/403/404/429/500/503 classifications, including the permission-denied 403 case | Outcome: must still pass unchanged | Stakeholder: regression coverage | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: file body, lines ~580-706 | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: run suite unmodified | Action: verify | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` | Role: test | Propagation: pins GraphQL-refusal 403 (no rate-limit wording) stays NON_RETRYABLE | Outcome: must still pass unchanged | Stakeholder: regression coverage | Owner: backlog_core maintainers | Evidence: file body | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: run suite unmodified | Action: