ay helper is deliberately shared here too. | Owner: implementer/architect for this item | Closure: an explicit scope decision \u2014 the item text names only `sync_state._classify_github_exception`, so confirm whether the GraphQL path is in or out of scope before closing.\\n- Unknown: whether a live GitHub primary-limit 403 response reliably includes `x-ratelimit-reset` in `GithubException.headers` in every deployment environment (proxies/gateways stripping headers). | Owner: implementer | Closure: the live e2e rate-limit test lane (`docs/live-e2e-validation.md`) or a captured real response; PyGithub's `Requester.createException` passes through the raw response headers unmodified (verified in `.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/Requester.py`), so the header is present whenever GitHub itself sends it, but this repo has not captured a live sample.\\n\\n### Transition, Rollback, and Observability\\n- Transition states: `SyncState.status` (IDLE/RUNNING/OFFLINE/ERROR) is unaffected as an enum; only which exceptions route to OFFLINE vs the retry branch changes. No stored schema or persisted format changes.\\n- Irreversible state and rollback limit: none \u2014 purely a classification/delay-computation change in process memory; reverting the diff fully restores prior behavior with no data migration.\\n- Leading indicators and failure signals: a malformed or over-large `x-ratelimit-reset` conversion would show up as either an immediate-retry storm (delay computed as 0/negative) or an anomalously long single sleep (delay computed as a huge future offset) in `sync_engine`'s \\\"Retrying in %.0f s\\\" log line.\\n- Paired baseline/candidate comparison: run `test_retryable_classification.py`, `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`, `test_startup_sync.py`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py`, and `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` before and after the change; only the new primary-limit case(s) should flip verdict, nothing else in those suites should change.\\n- Post-change review owner and trigger: backlog_core maintainer, triggered by any future report of a rate-limit-adjacent sync classification appearing wrong (mirroring how this item itself originated from Codex's finding on #3968).\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n- Overall system risk: MEDIUM\\n- Highest-risk systems: `backlog_core/sync_state.py::_classify_github_exception` and `::retry_after_seconds` (shared source of truth for every consumer); `backlog_core/sync_engine.py::_attempt_sync` (where a wrong delay value is most visible operationally); `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_rate_limit_retry_after.py` (only place the new epoch-to-delay arithmetic gets coverage).\\n- Main risk themes: (1) a single shared classifier fans out into 8+ call sites across sync, MCP tool responses, and backend read paths, so an over-broad or mis-ordered condition silently changes behavior everywhere at once; (2) the new `x-ratelimit-reset` to delay-seconds conversion is genuinely new arithmetic (unit/sign/clock-skew risk) with no existing test coverage to extend from.\\n- Scope expansion: yes \u2014 from the two RT-ICA-named locations (`sync_state._classify_github_exception`, `gh_client._is_rate_limited`) to the full consumer fan-out (`sync_engine.py`, `server.py`, `gh_client.py` x2, `backends/github_backend.py` x6) and the seven test files that pin adjacent, must-not-regress behavior.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n- [x] Every material propagation path has evidence or an explicit unknown\\n- [x] Producers, consumers, state owners, and downstream decisions checked\\n- [x] Tests, docs, config, CI, operations, and agent instructions checked\\n- [x] Applicable data, model, prompt, context, people, process, and control effects checked\\n- [x] Transition, rollback, delayed effects, and observability checked\\n- [x] Replacement or removal preserves the purpose of existing capabilities and controls\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193500Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None. The related prior work (#3968, #3969, #3975) is already merged; no open backlog item blocks this fix.\\n- Blocks: None identified among current open backlog items.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193403Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-27T23:15:14.974161Z \u2014 Add required Effort field for the output validation gate, value taken from the item's own Effort section</summary>\\n\\nP1 (unchanged from item priority) \u2014 a production-observed defect that removes both background sync and every backlog MCP tool's availability signal for the duration of any primary GitHub rate-limit window. Surfaced during the same live e2e / GitHub-robustness validation session as the already-merged #3968, #3969, #3975.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:15:14.974179Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-28T09:53:18.220957Z \u2014 Priority rationale unchanged by the scope addition; updating the Effort field line to match the re-estimated Effort (Small to Medium) recorded in the Effort section.</summary>\\n\\nP1 (unchanged from item priority) \u2014 a production-observed defect that removes both background sync and every backlog MCP tool's availability signal for the duration of any primary GitHub rate-limit window. Surfaced during the same live e2e / GitHub-robustness validation session as the already-merged #3968, #3969, #3975.\\n\\nEffort: Small\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-28T09:53:18.221005Z</sub>\\n\\nP1 (unchanged from item priority) \u2014 a production-observed defect that removes both background sync and every backlog MCP tool's availability signal for the duration of any primary GitHub rate-limit window. Surfaced during the same live e2e / GitHub-robustness validation session as the already-merged #3968, #3969, #3975.\\n\\nEffort: Medium\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193435Z</sub>\\n\\n- A primary-quota rate limit is temporary and resets on a