 both VERIFIED); 8 existing criteria retained verbatim</summary>\\n\\n1. Given a `GithubException` with status 403, headers lacking `Retry-After`, and a message indicating the primary quota is exhausted (GitHub's actual \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\" wording), the classifier returns `RETRYABLE` \u2014 verified by a new parametrized case in `test_retryable_classification.py` and/or a `sync_state` classification test.\\n2. Given that same exception carrying an `x-ratelimit-reset` header (epoch seconds in the future), the retry-delay computation returns a positive, bounded delay approximating `reset - now`; given a missing, non-numeric, or already-past `x-ratelimit-reset`, it returns a safe value (never negative, never absurdly large) \u2014 verified by new cases in `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`.\\n3. Given the same primary-limit 403 raised inside the sync loop, sync retries with a delay rather than setting `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` \u2014 verified by an integration-style test on `_attempt_sync`/`_sync_loop`.\\n4. Given the same primary-limit 403 surfaced through an MCP tool call, the tool's error response reports `retryable: true` with a `retry_after` value \u2014 verified by an added case in `test_retryable_classification.py`.\\n5. Negative case: `test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` (plain `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})`, no rate-limit wording) still passes unmodified \u2014 permission-denied 403s remain `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n6. Negative case: `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 the secondary-rate-limit branch is unaffected by the new primary-limit branch.\\n7. Negative case: `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 a GraphQL-refusal 403 with no rate-limit wording stays `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n8. Full existing suite for every touched or adjacent file (`test_startup_sync.py`, `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py`, `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py`, `test_sync_state_classify.py`, `test_retryable_classification.py`, `test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`) passes with no regressions.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:16:26.077272Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-28T09:48:09.048173Z \u2014 Repo owner added three related pre-existing issues (A: probe_backend_status 403 misclassification, B: missing retry= bound in make_github_client, C: backlog_view number:null) to this item's scope on 2026-09-28; adding criteria 10-12 for the added scope. Criteria 1-9 preserved verbatim \u2014 criterion 8 was checked against source (ls) and contains only bare filenames, no path, so no path correction was needed there.</summary>\\n\\n1. Given a `GithubException` with status 403, headers lacking `Retry-After`, and a message indicating the primary quota is exhausted (GitHub's actual \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\" wording), the classifier returns `RETRYABLE` \u2014 verified by a new parametrized case in `test_retryable_classification.py` and/or a `sync_state` classification test.\\n2. Given that same exception carrying an `x-ratelimit-reset` header (epoch seconds in the future), the retry-delay computation returns a positive, bounded delay approximating `reset - now`; given a missing, non-numeric, or already-past `x-ratelimit-reset`, it returns a safe value (never negative, never absurdly large) \u2014 verified by new cases in `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`.\\n3. Given the same primary-limit 403 raised inside the sync loop, sync retries with a delay rather than setting `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` \u2014 verified by an integration-style test on `_attempt_sync`/`_sync_loop`.\\n4. Given the same primary-limit 403 surfaced through an MCP tool call, the tool's error response reports `retryable: true` with a `retry_after` value \u2014 verified by an added case in `test_retryable_classification.py`.\\n5. Negative case: `test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` (plain `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})`, no rate-limit wording) still passes unmodified \u2014 permission-denied 403s remain `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n6. Negative case: `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 the secondary-rate-limit branch is unaffected by the new primary-limit branch.\\n7. Negative case: `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 a GraphQL-refusal 403 with no rate-limit wording stays `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n8. Full existing suite for every touched or adjacent file (`test_startup_sync.py`, `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py`, `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py`, `test_sync_state_classify.py`, `test_retryable_classification.py`, `test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`) passes with no regressions.\\n9. Given a GraphQL primary-rate-limit refusal (via `gh_client._graphql_request`) carrying an `x-ratelimit-reset` header and no `Retry-After` header, the raised `GitHubRateLimitedError` carries a positive, bounded retry delay derived from that reset time rather than falling back to no delay or an unbounded one \u2014 verified by a new case in the GraphQL rate-limit test coverage (e.g. `test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`).\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-28T09:48:09.048190Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given a `GithubException` with status 403, headers lacking `Retry-After`, and a message indicating the primary quota is exhausted (GitHub's actual \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\" wording), the classifier returns `RETRYABLE` \u2014 verified by a new parametrized case in `test_retryable_classification.py` and/or a `sync_state` classification test.\\n2. Given that same exception carrying an `x-ratelimit-reset` header (epoch seconds in the future), the retry-delay computation returns a positive, bounded delay approximating `reset - now`; given a missing, non-numeric, or already-past `x-ratelimit-reset`, it returns a safe value (never negative, never absurdly large) \u2014 verified by new cases in `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`.\\n3. Given the same primary-limit 403 raised inside the sync loop, sync retries with a delay rather than setting `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` \u2014 verified by an integration-style test on `_attempt_sync`/`_sync_loop`.\\n4. Given the same primary-limit 403 surfaced through an MCP tool call, the tool's error response reports `retryable: true` with a `retry_after` value \u2014 verified by an added case in `test_retryable_classification.py`.\\n5. Negative case: `test_startup_sync.py::test_403_without_retry_after_is_non_retryable` (plain `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})`, no rate-limit wording) still passes unmodified \u2014 permission-denied 403s remain `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n6. Negative case: `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 the secondary-rate-limit branch is unaffected by the new primary-limit branch.\\n7. Negative case: `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py` still passes unmodified \u2014 a GraphQL-refusal 403 with no rate-limit wording stays `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n8. Full existing suite for every touched or adjacent file (`test_startup_sync.py`, `test_rate_limit_retry_after.py`, `test_sync_state_secondary_rate_limit.py`, `test_sync_state_graphql_refusal.py`, `test_sync_state_classify.py`, `test_retryable_classification.py`, `test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`) passes with no regressions.\\n9. Given a GraphQL primary-rate-limit refusal (via `gh_client._graphql_request`) carrying an `x-ratelimit-reset` header and no `Retry-After` header, the raised `GitHubRateLimitedError` carries a positive, bounded retry delay derived from that reset time rather than falling back to no delay or an unbounded one \u2014 verified by a new case in the GraphQL rate-limit test coverage (e.g. `test_graphql_rate_limit_detection.py`).\\n10. Issue A \u2014 given a plain permission-denied 403 (no rate-limit header, no rate-limit wording in the body) raised while probing backend status, `probe_backend_status` does not report `BackendAvailability.RATE_LIMITED`; given a primary- or secondary-limit refusal (rate-limit header present, or body indicates a quota error), it does report `RATE_LIMITED`. Both cases are covered by tests on `probe_backend_status`.\\n11. Issue B \u2014 given a GitHub REST call that meets a primary rate limit, the call returns control to the caller within a bounded time instead of sleeping synchronously until `X-RateLimit-Reset`; the resulting failure is reported retryable and carries a `retry_after` delay, consistent with the classification behavior criteria 1-4 establish. Verified by a test that exercises `make_github_client`'s configured client against a simulated primary-limit 403 and asserts the call returns promptly rather than blocking.\\n12. Issue C \u2014 given a backlog item that has a GitHub issue number, `backlog_view` reports that number (not `null`) in its response, for both the `summary=True` and the over-budget `summary=False` response shapes. Reproduced as failing against #3978 before the fix (`\\\"number\\\": null` observed on a real over-budget `summary=False` call) and passing after.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193449Z</sub>\\n\\n- A REST 403 whose message/body indicates the primary quota is exhausted (GitHub's \\\"API rate limit exceeded\\\" wording), with or without a `Retry-After` header, is classified `RETRYABLE`, not `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n- The retry delay for that failure is derived from the `x-ratelimit-reset` header (epoch seconds, converted to a `now`-relative delay) when no `Retry-After` header is present, instead of yielding no delay hint.\\n- Background sync continues retrying, with the GitHub-supplied delay, instead of transitioning to `SyncStatus.OFFLINE` for this specific cause.\\n- MCP tool error responses for the same failure report `retryable: true` with a populated `retry_after`, instead of `retryable: false` with none.\\n- Already-correct classifications do not regress: secondary-rate-limit 403s (message contains \\\"secondary rate limit\\\") stay `RETRYABLE` via their existing path, and permission-denied 403s carrying no rate-limit signal at all (e.g. plain `GithubException(403, \\\"Forbidden\\\", {})`) stay `NON_RETRYABLE`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193512Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-28T09:51:28.929671Z \u2014 Re-estimated after repo owner added issues A-C to scope on 2026-09-28; original Small estimate covered only the primary-limit-403 classification fix.</summary>\\n\\nSmall \u2014 one classification branch plus one delay-conversion helper, both confined to `sync_state.py` as the sole producer. Downstream consumers (`sync_engine.py`, `server.py`, `gh_client.py`, `backends/github_backend.py`) need re-verification, not modification. Seven existing test files need extension or an unmodified re-run. Confidence is high: Fact-Check, Root-Cause Analysis, and a full Impact Radius mapping were already completed in prior grooming waves.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-28T09:51:28.929687Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 the original primary-limit-403 classification fix (`sync_state.py` branch + delay helper, seven test files) remains Small in isolation, but the three added issues raise the total. (A) is a small, isolated one-branch fix in `gh_client.py:probe_backend_status`, low risk. (C) is a small, two-file fix (add the same `result.issue`-parsing fallback `_build_compact_manifest` already uses to `_build_over_budget_view`, or populate `number` in `view_result_from_local_item`), low risk, root cause already identified. (B) is the driver of the increase \u2014 it requires deciding a bounded-retry replacement policy for `make_github_client`'s PyGithub client (an explicit `retry=` config vs. disabling PyGithub's built-in retry and routing 403s through the existing `sync_state` classifier), verifying no other call site relies on PyGithub's current silent-sleep behavior, and confirming the fix composes with the already-planned primary-limit classification change rather than duplicating or fighting it. Confidence remains high: all four issues (original scope plus A/B/C) now have verified root-cause file/line evidence; no MISSING RT-ICA condition blocks the added scope.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T23:09:50.193379Z</sub>\\n\\n1. In a checkout using the GitHub backend, exhaust the primary REST rate limit (`x-ratelimit-remaining` reaches 0) \u2014 either by making enough live GitHub API calls, or in a test by raising a `GithubException(403, {\\\"message\\\": \\\"API rate limit exceeded for ...\\\"}, headers)` with no `Retry-After` header.\\n2. Trigger the background sync loop, or call the `sync_now` MCP tool, while the primary limit is exhausted.\\n3. Observe `sync_status` transition to `OFFLINE` instead of continuing to retry.\\n4. Call any backlog MCP tool (e.g. `backlog_list`, `backlog_view`) during the same window and observe the error response's `retryable` field is `false` with no `retry_after` hint, even though GitHub's primary limit is temporary and self-clearing.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session 2026-09-27: live e2e / GitHub robustness work (#3968, #3969, #3975)\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-27\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-27T22:07:05.787494Z</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 5-whys via item description\\n**Evidence chain**:\\n1. Observed failure: RateLimitExceededException from GitHub primary-quota 403 is classified NON_RETRYABLE; sync transitions OFFLINE\\n2. Why: sync_state._classify_github_exception matches the HTTP 403 status to NON_RETRYABLE\\n3. Why: No RateLimitExceededException-specific classification check exists before the generic 403 rule\\n4. Why: Classification logic orders generic patterns (status codes) before specific exception types\\n5. Why: Code was written with broad-match-first strategy instead of narrow-before-broad exception hierarchy\\n**Root cause**: Exception classification uses generic-before-specific pattern matching, allowing temporary rate-limit 403s to be misclassified as permanent auth failures.\\n**Fix locus**: sync_state._classify_github_exception, 403 branch \u2014 add RateLimitExceededException check before generic 403 rule; extract delay from x-ratelimit-reset when Retry-After absent\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-28T09:54:40.435150Z</sub>\\n\\n- Issue A (added 2026-09-28 by repo owner): `probe_backend_status` in `gh_client.py` treats every 403 as rate-limited, contradicting `_classify_github_exception`'s permission-denied vs. rate-limit split.\\n- Issue B (added 2026-09-28 by repo owner): `make_github_client` passes no `retry=` to PyGithub, so a primary rate limit blocks a REST call synchronously for up to ~1 hour instead of surfacing a bounded, retryable failure.\\n- Issue C (added 2026-09-28 by repo owner): `backlog_view` returns `\\\"number\\\": null` for an item that has a GitHub issue number (observed live on #3978 itself).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"47ec14ebdce30bda33c2eb142d9beca12116423ec9139f46bc8704122eef9461\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABXcxsEw\",\"fields\":{\"stack\":\"\",\"status\":\"in-progress\",\"item_type\":\"Bug\",\"priority\":\"P1\",\"added\":\"2026-09-27\",\"files\":\"\",\"followup_to\":\"\",\"layer\":\"\",\"research_first\":\"\",\"close_reference\":\"\",\"source\":\"Session 2026-09-27: live e2e / GitHub robustness work (#3968, #3969, #3975)\",\"groomed\":\"2026-09-28\",\"topic\":\"\",\"close_comment\":\"\",\"plan\":\"#3978,P999000ed\",\"language\":\"\",\"suggested_location\":\"\",\"close_reason\":\"\"}}"}